\subsubsection{A short note about some other cases}

Overt violations of the CSC are also reported in other configurations. For example, such violations are reported in German and discussed in \cite{Johnson:2002} (and analyzed in a way similar to some of the A-movement CSC violations discussed in section \ref{Amov}) and in \cite{mayr2017asymmetric}. Similarly, overt violations have long been reported  e.g. in South Slavic although this is debated (cf. \citealp{arsenijevic2020experimenting}, and references therein) to some of which we briefly return below in section \ref{cscbsection}.   \\
Additional work, possibly more radical in its implications that I  do not discuss here, argues that (covert) QR violations of the CSC occur in cases of `telescoping' in which certain quantifiers seem to bind across conjunctions, disjunction and even sentence boundaries, see \cite{keshet2007telescoping}.\\
% 
%\cite{kato2006symmetries} also discusses 
How to analyze these cases is still controversial. They seem consistent with what is concluded in section \ref{formul}, but only further research can determine if they truly are.

\subsection{The A-movement case: \cite{Lin:2001}, \cite{Lin:2002}, \cite{Johnson:2017gapping}}\label{Amov}

Clear cases of A-movement violating the Coordinate Structure Constraint are found with Gapping constructions. This is discussed in various works of Kyle Johnson's since the early 1990's, see  \cite{Johnson:2017gapping} for a summary, as well as in \cite{Lin:2001}, \cite{Lin:2002}, which, adopting the view in \cite{Ruys:1992} and  \cite{Fox:2000} concludes that the CSC is an interpretive constraint. Here is one case illustrating these violations (and a couple more are discussed below in section \ref{formul}).

Consider the following from \citet[(88)-(90)]{Johnson:2017gapping}:
\ex.  \a.  X can be true and Y be false \label{88}
\a. because they are logically independent \label{88a}
\b. \# but X can't be true if Y is false.\label{88b}
\z.
\b. It's possible for X to be true and Y to be false (because they are logically independent).  \label{89}
\b.  X can be true and Y can be false (but X can't be true if Y is false).\\ \label{90}
{\it compare}\\
\# It's possible for X to be true and Y to be false but X can't be true if Y is false.

\ref{88} is unambiguous in a surprising way: it must mean that it is possible both for X to be true and Y to be false. More precisely, \ref{88} can express what \ref{89} does, and is therefore compatible with the continuation in \ref{88a}.
This is the interpretation that arises if {\it can} outscopes {\it and}. But \ref{88}  can't express what \ref{90} does, and is therefore, unlike \ref{90}, incompatible with the continuation in \ref{88b}.
This means that  the modal {\it can} must have scope over the conjunct in cases like \ref{88} (see \citealp{Johnson:2017gapping} for a discussion of when this arises).

What kind of syntax gives rise to this pattern? The syntax in  \ref{91a} below with clausal coordination would give the wrong result since it would allow the interpretation in \ref{90}. 
\ex. \a. X can be true and Y \sout{can} be false \label{91a}
\b. X$_k$ can [ [ be t$_k$ true] and [ Y be false] ] \label{91b}

The syntax in \ref{91b} - the small conjunct analysis - correctly yields only the right one and is thus widely adopted (e.g. by \citealp{Siegel:1984} in essence, \citealp{Coppock:2001}, \citealp{Lin:2002}, \citealp{Johnson:2017gapping},  \citealp{potter2017two}, \citealp{hirsch2017inflexible}). But this requires A-movement to be able to violate the CSC.

Before concluding this section, note that another conceivable derivation not violating the CSC is not available. This derivation for an analog of  \ref{88}, would take the following form: 
\ex. This$_k$ can't be t$_k$ true and that$_m$ \sout{can} be t$_m$ false

It would involve coordination of two full clauses, with raising to subject in both conjuncts and require mandatory total reconstruction in both. Of course why total reconstruction would be required would have to somehow be derived. But such an approach runs into problems because total reconstruction of a raised subject is in fact not required. This is discussed in the next section, see examples \ref{103} and \ref{NPI}.  


\section{How to formulate the CSC}\label{formul}

The CSC clearly does not block movement itself, neither in the A-bar movement case, not in the A movement case but seems instead to constrain the output of movement, as  \cite{Ruys:1992} had concluded. How should it be formulated? 
The discussion in this section is limited to CSCa \ref{csca}: extraction from within a conjunct.\\
\citet[chap 2, (57)] {Fox:2000} adopts the following which would be consistent with the observed data on wh-movement and QR:

\ex. \label{CSCF} \a. Extraction out of a coordinate structure is possible only when the structure consists of two independent substructures, each composed of one the coordinates together with material above it up to the landing site (henceforth, {\it component structures}. 
 \b. Grammatical constraints are checked independently in each of the component structures.

It would apply as follows. In a licit, ATB movement case, each component is well formed.
\ex. ATB WH-movement
\a. $\checkmark$ Which poet$_k$ did you [ [ read t$_k$] and [ love t$_k$] ]? \label{lov}\\
Component Structures:
\b. $\checkmark$ Which poet$_k$ did you  [ read t$_k$]?
\b. $\checkmark$ Which poet$_k$ did you [ love t$_k$ ]? 

In an illicit non ATB movement case, one component is ill formed, ruled out by  the independent general principle in \ref{vq}:
\ex. Non-ATB WH-movement
\a. * Which poet$_k$ did you [ [ read t$_k$] and [ love William Blake] ]?\\
Component Structures:
\b. $\checkmark$ Which poet$_k$ did you  [ read t$_k$] ?
\b. * Which poet$_k$ did you [ love William Blake ]? 

\ex. Vacuous quantification is banned \label{vq}

In a licit non ATB movement case, with a bound pronoun, there is no vacuous quantification.

\ex. Non-ATB WH-movement
\a.  $\checkmark$  Which poet$_k$ did you [ [ read t$_k$] and [ love all of his$_k$ poems ] ]? \label{100a}\\
Component Structures:
\b. $\checkmark$ Which poet$_k$ did you  [ read t$_k$] ?
\b.   $\checkmark$ Which poet$_k$ did you [ love all of his$_k$ poems ]? \label{100c}

To derive the right result here, we must make a bit more precise what is meant by `grammatical constraints' in \ref{CSCF}.  Indeed \ref{100c} on its own is less acceptable than \ref{100a}. A natural idea is to restrict them to LF relevant constraints, that is interpretive constraints. This is in part independently  warranted (see below example \ref{mismatch} showing that agreement mismatch does not matter). From this point of view, \ref{100c} is well formed but degraded for other non interpretive reasons.\footnote{That is whatever governs the availability of resumptive pronouns, for example, competition with alternatives lacking a resumptive, which would be responsible for why a pronoun in the trace position in the second conjunct of \ref{lov} is perceived as somewhat deviant.}
As  \cite{Lin:2002}, proposes, this applies to the A-movement case just discussed. Accordingly,  an example  like \ref{91b}  or \ref{101} below would be well formed provided that the subject {\it Bill} totally reconstructs in its trace position as in \ref{101a}, yielding the two components \ref{101b} and \ref{101c}, each interpretively well formed:\footnote{As \cite{Lin:2002} notes, total reconstruction of {\it Bill} seems to contradict Fox's 2000 Scope Economy condition barring vacuous scope shifting operation. This could be taken to mean that scope independent elements such as proper names do not fall under Scope Economy.}
\ex. Bill$_k$ can't [ [ be t$_k$ right] and [ Tom be wrong] ] \label{101}
\a.   can't [ [ be Bill$_k$ right] and [ Tom be wrong] ] \label{101a}
\a. can't  [ be Bill$_k$ right]  \label{101b}
\b. can't [ Tom be wrong]  \label{101c}
\z.
\b. Bill$_k$  can't [ [ be t$_k$ right] and [ Tom be wrong] ] \label{101aa}
\a. Bill$_k$ can't  [ be t$_k$ right]  \label{101ba}
\b. Bill$_k$ can't [ Y be wrong]  \label{101ca}
\z.

Failing to totally reconstruct the subject Bill in its trace position as in \ref{101aa} would yield the two components \ref{101ba} and \ref{101ca}, the former interpretively ill formed (Bill not being the argument of anything).   
As  \cite{Lin:2002} remarks this makes two correct predictions.\\
First, the following sentence is ambiguous, but its gapping counterpart is not:
\ex. \a. Many drummers can't leave on Friday \label{102a}
\b. Many drummers can't leave on Friday, and many guitarists arrive on Saturday \label{102b}

The ambiguity of \ref{102a} arise because the subject may either scope over or under the negated modal. If the subject outscopes the modal, the sentence means that for many different individual drummers, it's the case that they are unable to leave on Friday. If the subject is interpreted below the negated modal, this yields:  it is not possible that a large group of drummers leave on Friday.\\ 
The above account correctly predict that only this second reading is available in \ref{102b}, where {\it many drummers} reconstructs under the negated modal. \\

That non ATB movement from the first conjunct is allowed when there is  a bound pronoun in the second, hence without vacuous quantification, takes care of examples inspired by \citet[p.248, (15a)]{McCawley:1993} and his discussion, and also discussed in  \citet[p.73(23)]{Lin:2002}. Note first that the well known well-formedness of {\it Noone$_k$'s mother scolded him$_k$}, shows that a possessor can scope like its DP container. 

\ex. No one$_k$'s duck will [ [t be moist enough] or [his$_k$ mussels be tender enough] ] \label{103}
\a. No one$_k$ will  [t$_k$'duck  be moist enough]  \label{103a}
\b. No one$_k$ will  [his$_k$ mussels be tender enough] \label{103b}

The remarkable fact  here is that the QP {\it no one'} in the first conjunct is able to bind the pronoun {\it his} in the second conjunct.  First, this means, again, that we cannot be dealing with two coordinated full clauses (with ellipisis), since, as McCawley notes, the following sentence is ill formed as the pronoun is not in the scope of the quantifier:
\ex. * [No one$_k$'s duck will be moist enough] or /and his$_i$ mussels \sout{will} be tender enough.

Second, the subject outscopes the coordination as shown in \ref{103}: the sentence only means that there is nobody who is such that his duck will be moist enough and his mussels tender enough. This time, this option is predicted to be fine: the two components that such a sentence yields without reconstruction, namely \ref{103a}  and \ref{103b}, are both interpretively well formed, the QP in each of them binding a variable.\footnote{Combining the last two types of examples shows that the syntactic structure must be able to be a bit more complex than shown so far. Indeed, consider the French example (which apparently differs from comparable English examples reported in \citet[p.81, (43b)]{Lin:2002}):
\vspace{-1em}
\exg.[(i)] {Beaucoup de} musiciens {ne peuvent pas} partir le jeudi et leurs rempla\c cants n'arriver que le samedi\\  
Many musicians$_k$ can't leave on Thursday, and their$_k$ replacements arrive only on Saturday\\ 

\vspace{-1em}
Given the presence of the pronoun in the second conjunct, reconstruction of {\it many musicians}
under the negated modal should not be required. But this contradicts at least my judgement: the subject must scope under the modal. This shows that the subject must reconstruct below the modal but not so low as not to take the coordinated structure in its scope. This can be resolved if the structure is as below, with an intermediate trace on the spine, outside of the coordinated structure:
\vspace{-1em}
\ex.[(ii)] Many musicians$_k$  [can't  {\red t$_k$}  [[ t$_k$ leave on Friday] and [their$_k$ replacements  arrive only on Saturday]] 


} 
This also takes care of the following kind of contrast discussed in  \citet[p.74 (35)]{Lin:2002}. Consider first:

\ex. \label{NPI} \a. No girl$_k$ will eat a green banana, or any of her$_{\checkmark k, *m}$ friends drink a pureed one. 
\b. * No girl will eat a green banana, or any boy drink a pureed one. \label{NPI2}

In the first example, just like in the case of CSC violations with wh-movement or QR, the possibility for the QP {\it no girl} to bind the pronoun {\it her} in the second conjunct allows a CSC violation. As a result, the QP {\it no girl} can outscope the disjunction and license the NPI {\it any of her friends} (only if {\it her} is bound by {\it no girl}.
This yields the following logical form:
\ex.   No girl$_k$ will [ [ t$_k$ eat a green banana]  or [ any of her$_{\checkmark k, *m}$ friends drink a pureed one]]

In the absence of a bound pronoun in the second disjunct as in \ref{NPI2}, the subject must totally reconstruct into the VP: as a result, the NPI {\it any boy} is no longer in its scope and is thus unlicensed, yielding deviance. Once again, this contrast is unexpected if we were dealing with a conjunction of clauses.

We may wonder why Fox's generalization in \ref{CSCF} should hold? Why should each component independently be checked for LF well formedness, at least for the cases we have looked at? Recent work may suggest a natural answer. \cite{schein2017and} and \cite{hirsch2017inflexible}  defend in different ways an analysis of at least some cases of symmetric coordination (of the type discussed here) as  involving conjunction reduction  in the classical sense of taking arguments with (more or less) sentence meanings. As a result, each component must be checked for interpretability because these components are syntactically and semantically present (together with some ellipsis and its effects).\\  
This said, Fox's generalization is good enough for our purposes here but is too coarse to handle all cases of (even symmetric) coordination. Many more questions arise than can even be hinted at here, regarding coordinations, the relation with plurality, with distributivity, cumulativity  and so on (see e.g. \citealt{schmitt2019pluralities}, \citealt{schmitt2020boolean}).\footnote{The relevance of these considerations is illustrated by such examples as:
(i) John and Jean were admiring pictures of a black cat$_m$ and pictures of the dog next to it$_m$
(ii) Which cat$_m$ were John and Jean admiring pictures of t$_m$ and pictures of the dog next to it$_m$?

Sentence (i) is true in a context in which John is only admiring pictures of a black cat and Jean is only admiring pictures of the next to the black cat. Sentence (ii) allows this kind of cumulative reading and is not a CSC violation. It is not clear how to get the components  needed for Fox's generalization, namely {\it Which cat$_m$ was John admiring pictures of t$_m$?} and {\it Which cat$_m$ was Jean admiring  pictures of the dog next to it$_m$?}. The formulation in \ref{CSCform} handles this type of sentence straighforwardly. } A slightly more abstract characterization would take the following form suggested in {\cite{Johnson:2009a} fundamentally limiting it to \ref{CSCform}:
\ex. Coordinate Structure Constraint \label{CSCform}\\
In a string  containing a coordination, if binding into one conjunct is required for computing the interpretation, binding into to all conjuncts is required.

For example a moved wh-phrase cannot be interpreted without binding its trace. Then this phrase will have to bind something in each conjuncts. Similarly,  since  standardly A-moved DP will need to bind its trace to compute its $\theta$-role, this DP will have to bind something in each conjuncts. It should be clear that Fox's formulation is a subcase of this constraint where each components is interpretable by itself, something that is not necessarily the case (and underlies the debates regarding how to treat say conjunction, as Boolean or not).

The idea that the CSC is a constraint on interpretation makes it also apt to handle apparent CSC violations involved in asymmetric coordination (regardless of what the coordinator is, be it {\it and, or, etc..}) of the following kind: 
\ex. \a. How much can you [drink \_ and still stay sober]? (\citet[example 2]{Lakoff:1986})
\b.  How many lakes can we [destroy \_ and not arouse public antipathy]? (\citet[p. 201]{Pollard:1994})
\b. He regards the limitless abundance of language as its most important property, one that any theory of language [must account for \_ or be discarded]. (\citet[p. 183]{campbell1982grammatical})

Indeed, characterizing such cases requires paying attention to the interpretive properties of the constructions. Thus a necessary condition for these type of violations to be allowed is failure of semantic symmetry defined as  truth conditional invariance under conjunct permutation (cf. \citealp{mayr2017asymmetric}, for discussion).\footnote{That a semantic property is a prerequisite casts doubts on pragmatic, rather than semantic,  treatments of the CSC as in \cite{kubota2015coordinate}.} Thus the formulation in \ref{CSCform} could be restricted to symmetric coordination, precisely because it's very symmetry is at the core of the requirement of `equal treatment' of the conjuncts.

Finally, note that we have not discussed  head movement. How to model head movement is controversial. What matters here is whether it could have interpretive effects. If not, it is predicted to be able to always violate the (classical) CSC. If yes, as some authors (e.g. \citealp{lechner2006interpretive}, \citealp{Roberts:2010}, \citealp{harizanov2019whither}) argue, the prediction of the present account, to be verified, is that these interpretive effects should not arise when head movement violates the  (classical) CSC (see below for one possible case).

\section{The Conjunct Constraint}\label{cscbsection}

We now briefly return to clause CSCb in \ref{cscb}, the Conjunct constraint, prohibiting movement of one of the conjuncts, for example as in:
\ex. \a. * We know the people  who$_i$ Henry wanted to meet [$_{DP}$ t$_i$ ] and [$_{DP}$ friends of t$_i$/James ]
\b. * We know the people  who$_i$ Henry wrote to [$_{DP}$ t$_i$ ] and [$_{DP}$  t$_i$/James ]
\b. * We know the people who$_i$ Henry wrote to  [$_{DP}$  t$_i$/James ] and [$_{DP}$ t$_i$ ]


In languages like English (or French), this constraint cannot be overtly violated. As shown, extraction of either conjunct is ill formed, and remarkably,  ATB extraction does not rescue such sentences from deviance, thus suggesting as previously argued  that \ref{csca} and \ref{cscb} are distinct constraints.  Reportedly, overt violations are fine in some other languages, cf.,  \cite{Oda:2021}, and references therein, but only involving extraction of the first conjunct. This raises questions as to how CSCb in \ref{cscb} applies. 
First we may ask whether covert movement can violate the \ref{cscb} in English or French. The answer is negative. Consider:
\ex. \ag. Quelqu'un {a emprunté} au moins deux  livres et au moins un magazine.\\
 Someone borrowed at least two books and at least one magazines.\\

It is possible for the subject to outscope the whole conjunction yielding {\it there is someone who borrowed at least two books and a magazine}. It is also possible for the whole conjunction to outscope the subject yielding {\it if there are at least two books and at least one magazine such that each of them has been borrowed by someone possibly different for each item}. For example for each of War and  Peace, Anna Karenina and Newsweek, there is a different borrower. What is not allowed however is split scope: the first conjunct cannot outscope the subject with the second conjunct remaining in the scope of the subject. If this were possible, it would yield the meaning: {\it there are two books, such that  for each of them x, a different person borrowed x and at least one possibly different magazine}. That is, in a situation where Anna borrowed War and Peace and Newsweek, and Elsa borrowed Anna Karenina and The Economist, this sentence would be judged true if this reading was available. But it is not.\\
This means that at least in English and French, clause CSCb must hold of covert movement, at LF, as well as of overt movement.

Interestingly the covert movement prohibition appears to hold at least in some Serbian varieties that may allow overt violations of CSCb (the exact status and analysis of the b examples below is debated, hence the ? rating, but to the extent they are acceptable, they do not allow split scope).\footnote{Thanks to Boban Arsenijevic and to Milica Denic for their input here.} Thus consider the following examples: 
\ex. \ag.  Neko je kupio makar tri knjige i makar sedam filmova\\
Somebody aux buy-past-part at-least 3 books and at-least 7 movies\\
\bg.  ? Makar tri knjige je neko i makar sedam filmova kupio\\
at-least 3 books aux somebody and at least 7 movies buy-past-part\\
'Somebody bought at least three books and at least seven movies.'

We find that the same readings are permitted and excluded as in English and French:\\
The following reading is fine for both examples: {\it somebody $>$ at least 3 books and at least 7 movies.}\\
And so is this one:  {\it at least 3 books and at least 7 movies $>$ somebody}.\\
But the `split scope' reading is not available for either: {\it * at least 3 books x are such that there is y who bought x and at least 7 movies.}\\ 
Similarly:
\ex. \ag. Ako Marko neki doma\'ci ili neki ispit ne uradi, pa\v{s}\'ce godinu\\
if Marko some homework or some exam not does, fail-fut-3sg year\\
\bg. ?Ako neki doma\'ci Marko ili neki ispit ne uradi, pa\v{s}\'ce godinu\\
if some homework Marko or some exam not does, fail-fut-3sg year\\
'If Marko doesn't do some of the homework assignments or some of the exams, he will fail the year'.

The following reading is fine for both: {\it if $>$ some homework or some exam $>$ not}, that is,  he has to do all homework and all exams to not fail the year.\\
So is this one: {\it if $>$ not $>$ some homework or some exam}, that is, he has to do at least some homework or at least some exam to not fail the year.\\
But the following `split scope' reading is not available for either: If there is a homework assignment x, such that Marko doesn't do x or some of the exams, he will fail the year. (i.e., Marko has to do all homework assignments or some of the exams to not fail the year).\\

Of course, these are preliminary data that would need to be more systematically investigated.  But we can tentatively  draw the following conclusions on their basis.
\begin{enumerate}[noitemsep]
\item First, clause CSCb in \ref{cscb} holds at LF. This parallels what we concluded for clause CSCa in \ref{csca}.\\  
\item Second,  since QR of at least these quantifiers (such as {\it at least}) obey island constraints,\footnote{Not all do, or not all do in the same way. {\it each} is well known to be much freer than {\it every} or {\it at least}. As far as I know, why different quantifiers behave differently is not understood.} and in particular clause CSCa, the overt/covert difference between English and French on the one hand and Serbian (and possibly other South Slavic languages) if indeed there is one, on the other does not appear to be structural in nature. Rather it would be an overt/covert difference of the EPP type: English and French require an overt first conjunct, whereas Serbian may tolerate a silent first conjunct. 
\end{enumerate}
If first conjuncts can move overtly, a debated assumption as noted, the most general conclusion would be that a first conjunct can in principle always move, but only covertly in English and French. Furthermore, if it moves alone, it must mandatorily totally reconstruct.\footnote{A covert movement mandatorily reconstructing would differ from no movement at all in its ability to license e.g. agreement in a spec head configuration.}  If first conjunct movement is tolerated but must totally reconstruct, it would exemplify with A-bar movement a configuration similar to what is found with A-movement in Gapping and discussed earlier: overt movement with mandatory total reconstruction. 
%
%
%Consider:
%
%\exg.  \label{cov}Au moins un satellite {a atteint} chaque {orbite cible} et sa {vitesse de croisière}\\
%At least one satellite reached every {target orbit}  and its {cruising speed}\\
%
%Judgments are difficult, but this sentence seems to allow a reading whereby for each orbit x, at least one satellite t reached x and t's cruising speed. For this reading to be allowed, the first conjunct must outscope the subject, with the second conjunct remaining in the scope of the subject to allow its pronoun to be bound as shown. This means that QR, which we did show earlier does obey \ref{csca}, can violate \ref{cscb}. The difference between languages would thus not be a matter of structural differences but an overt /covert difference. How exactly to capture this variation thus remains an unsettled question. Assume for now some arbitrary property akin to the EPP property: English and French {\it and} has an EPP feature mandating the presence of an overt first conjunct, which languages violating \ref{cscb} lack.\\
Note finally that no language is reported to allow violations of clause CSCb in \ref{cscb} by overtly extracting the second conjunct, a prohibition requiring a different explanation but puzzingly similar to what is observed with respect to clause CSCa: in the latter, extraction from the second conjunct binding a pronoun inside the first conjunct is ill formed, even if  it meets condition \ref{CSCform} and movement is of a kind not triggering Weak Crossover effects.  
