\section{Consequences}

\paragraph{Binding:} Some authors (\citealp{Kayne:2002}, \citealp{drummond2011minimalist}, \citealp{charnavelsportiche:2022,charnavelsportiche:2021a,charnavelsportiche:2023a}) have argued that the relation between an anaphor and is antecedent is one of movement of, or from, inside the anaphor.  Examine first the case of  movement of a constituent smaller than a whole conjunct. If it is movement, it must be allowed to violate the CSC as a constraint on movement:
\ex. \linkto{The Orsay museum$_k$} \under sells replicas [ [of the Louvre] ] and [of itself \from t$_k$ ] ] 

Would a derivation involving movement  violates the CSC \ref{csca} as a constraint on interpretation?  The answer is negative.\\
The components given by the formulation of the CSC in \ref{CSCF} would be:

\ex. \a. The Orsay museum$_k$ sells replicas of the Louvre 
\b. The Orsay museum$_k$  sells replicas of [ itself t$_k$  ] 

Both are well formed.  Precisely because such a derivation would involve movement of the DP {\it the Orsay museum} to a $\theta$-position, this DP is able, but, crucially, is not required, to bind anything (unlike in standard A-movement, where binding of a trace is needed to get a $\theta$-role).\\
Consider next the case of movement of an anaphor being a whole conjunct:
\ex. \linkto{The Orsay museum$_k$} \under sells replicas of [ [\from itself$_k$ ] and [ the Louvre ]]

Whether this violates the CSC, here CSCb, would depend on the particular of the analysis but if movement is required for `formal' reasons only, we would be dealing with covert movement totally reconstructing, which would not violate \ref{cscb}. This said, anaphors are not restricted to appear as the first conjunct. Inverting {\it the Louvre} and {\it itself} above preserves well formedness. If totally reconstructing covert movement of the second conjunct (or more generally of conjuncts that are not first) were prohibited (as it may well be), this would constitute an argument against the assumption that the whole anaphor moves in movement approaches to anaphor binding, but not to movement approaches in general.
 
Now, some analyses (\citealp{Anagnostopoulou:1999a}, \citealp{spathas:2010}, \citealp{lechner:2012}, \citealp{patel2013principle}, \citealp{sauerland2013presuppositions}) invoke {\it self}-movement for reflexive binding, a potential case of head movement, as e.g. in:
\ex. \a. John$_i$ hurt him$_i$self $\rightarrow$ 
\b. John$_i$ self-hurt him$_i$

 which affects the meaning of the verb by turning it into a reflexive verb (roughly turning a dyadic $\lambda x. \lambda y. P(x,y)$ into a monadic $\lambda x. P(x,x)$). This meaning changing movement predicts, wrongly, that such sentences as {\it The hammer$_k$ damaged [ the nail and itself$_k$} ] should be ill formed as LF violations of the CSC (that is of \ref{csca}, as this would be extraction from inside a conjunct). Some assumption must therefore be wrong: one possibility could be that such sentences do not have to involve {\it self} movement (although this undermines the appeal to such movement in the first place); the other is that {\it self} movement is simply not an option, as \citealp{angelopoulos2023treating} or \citealp{sportiche:2022} conclude.

\paragraph{Control:} It should be clear that exactly the same reasoning makes \citeauthor{Hornstein:1999}'s \citeyear{Hornstein:1999} analysis of Obligatory Control as movement immune to the CSC. Returning to example \ref{control}, its components are as given, and both well formed:\footnote{Note incidentally that this is true even if movement completely vacates one of the conjuncts (see footnote \ref{vac}). This would also hold in the binding case above if, as argue, the reflexive is the trace (as opposed to containing the trace of) of its antecedent. }

\ex. \linkto Mary \under {wants} [ [ \from PRO {to win} ] and [ John {to lose} ] ] 
\a. Mary {wants} [ PRO {to win} ]
\b. Mary {wants} {} John {to lose} ] 

The CSC is thus not relevant to decide the feasibility of this approach to control.


\paragraph{Clitic Doubling}  \cite{paparounas2023first} references a situation in Greek in which a clitic  here cl$^1_k$ doubles the first conjunct of a coordination of direct objects, here DP$_1$ as below:

\ex. ... cl$^1$ ... [$_{DP}$ [$_{DP_1}$ X] and [$_{DP_2}$ Y] ] \label{cldbl}

Would a movement approach to cliticization violate the CSC?\\
Movement would either be of the clitic alone as in BigDP approaches (where the clitic forms a constituent [ cl DP] with the DP it doubles prior to moving), or would involve covert movement of DP$_1$  to an independently generated cl$^1_k$  ( \citealt{Sportiche1996}, \citealt{Angelopoulos2020}) as in \citealp{Angelopoulos:2019}. 
%Given our conclusion that  the CSC (both CSCa in \ref{}csca} and CScb in \ref{cscb}) is an LF condition, whether the CSC is violated depends on how the resulting structure is interpreted.
%
%.  This would yield the following structure:%, with its components once total reconstruction has applied (as it would have to, else one component will be ill formed):  
% 
%\ex. \linkto{ [$_{DP_1}$ X]}  \under cl$^1$ ... [$_{DP}$ [$_{DP_1}$ \from t ] and [$_{DP_2}$ Y] ] \label{cldbl}
%\a. ... cl$^1$ ...  [$_{DP_1}$ X] 
% \b. ... cl$^1$ ... [$_{DP_2}$ Y] 
% 
%Would this violate the CSC as an LF condition, that is, are these interpretively well formed? It all depends on the status of the clitic. 
If the clitic has some an interpretive property and does not (or cannot) totally reconstruct, ill formedness would be predicted. If the clitic  totally reconstructs at LF (as it could in a BigDP approach), no violation would ensue. Furthermore, \cite{Angelopoulos2020} argues that such clitics in Greek (or in French) do not contribute any semantics: they are probes agreeing with their goals the way T agrees with its goal which means that they are from an interpretive point of view invisible, even with some $\phi$ features or Case values specified. These would behave like the features on the verb {\it be} in the following French Gapping case, which translate the English examples \ref{101} with the same interpretation (requiring total reconstruction of the subject). They are well formed despite the mismatch in $\phi$ features:
 
\exg. Ces propositions {ne peuvent pas} \^etre vraies et celle-ci \^etre fausse \label{mismatch}\\
	these propositions can't-3$^{rd}$prs-plural be true and  {this one} be false\\
\ag.   {ne peuvent pas} [ces propositions \^etre vraies] \\
	can't-3$^{rd}$prs-plural [these propositions  be true] \\ 
\bg.  {ne peuvent pas} [celle-ci \^etre fausse] \\
	can't-3$^{rd}$prs-plural [{this one} be false] \\

In sum, under either of these options, there would be no CSC violation.
%In other words, these components would be interpretively well formed.\\
%\footnote{Interestingly, there are cases of agreement which are interpreted, in the sense that the agreement configuration has to hold at LF. Interestiingly, in such cases, une moitié d'entre nous devons être loyaux et l'autre être déloyale} 
%Under an approach such as that of  the clitic is generated high and, in clitic doubling, its associate DP raises to it covertly (see \citealp{Angelopoulos:2019}). This would be covert movement of a first conjunct which escapes the conjunct condition \ref{cscb}. The fact that only the first conjunct can be so doubled is consistent with what is observed in violations of \ref{cscb}.

This means that an argument against movement being involved with Clitic Doubling based on putative CSC violations is not convincing, for now. Nor is  the particular argument against big DP approach to the syntax of clitics in \cite{Angelopoulos2020} referenced on page \pageref{AnSpo}. In fact, the observation that such clitic doubling can only target the first conjunct parallels the behavior of overt conjunct movement in South Slavic, which can only target the first conjunct.\\
Interestingly,  \cite{Angelopoulos2020} leaves open the status of Dative clitics, mentioning that they may have interpretive import (e.g. possibly animacy, or inducing affectedness on their associate). If they do, the expectation would be that one of the corresponding components in a situation like \ref{cldbl} would be ill-formed. Preliminary consultations with native speakers of Greek suggest this is a correct prediction. This would fall out under the approach in \cite{Sportiche1996}; it would also under a BigDP approach if it can be shown that the clitic cannot totally reconstruct.
 
\paragraph{Other Remarks} First, that movement can violate the CSC has implications for certain formalisms treating coordination, and the CSC in terms of unification of features. To preserve a unification approach, they will have to treat traces (or their equivalent) and bound pronouns in similar ways. \\
Second, if the CSC can be violated by A-bar or A-movement, deviance appearing to be tied to violations of the CSC by movement alone, if there are some, will raise challenging analytical questions. 

%\paragraph{Agree:} There is a debate about the syntactic configuration under which agreement is triggered. S/Users/ds/Desktop/impots 2023 USome (e.g. \cite{koopman:2005a}) defend the idea that agreement is only triggered in a spec/head configurations, while others (e.g. \cite{Chomsky:2001a}) propose that a probe can agree with a goal it c-commands. Perhaps the strongest argument for the latter position comes from configurations of first conjunct agreement, a relatively common phenomenon, very much resembling the clitic doubling configurations above.
%
%\ex. ... \linkto Probe \under .. [$_{DP}$ [$_{DP_1}$ \from Goal ] and [$_{DP_2}$ Y ] ]
%
%An analysis in terms of covert ellipsis or coordinated clauses with a repeated (but elided) probe  ({\it Probe... DP$_1$ and \sout{Probe}... DP$_2$} as in \cite{Aoun:1994} appearing not to always available, a spec head approach to agreement would require DP$_1$ to raise to the spec of Probe to license agreement. But this would violate the CSC as a constraint on movement. 
